.TH MKSCRIPT 1 "2026-08-10" "mkscript 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mkscript \- create executable Bash script skeletons safely
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkscript
[\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-strict\fR]
[\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.I path
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkscript
creates an executable Bash script at the requested path.
By default it writes only the standard Bash shebang:
.PP
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env bash
.EE
.PP
When
.B \-s
or
.B \-\-strict
is supplied,
.B mkscript
also writes:
.PP
.EX
set -euo pipefail
.EE
.PP
Existing files, symlinks, and directories are never overwritten.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-strict
Add
.B set -euo pipefail
after the shebang.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display usage information and exit successfully.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display the program version and exit successfully.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 64
Command-line usage error.
.TP
.B 73
The target script could not be created safely.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Create a simple script in the current directory:
.EX
mkscript hello-world
.EE
.TP
Create a strict-mode script:
.EX
mkscript --strict deploy.sh
.EE
